  "structural_zeros": [
    {
      "cell": "QUERY label TRUE",
      "reason": "With exactly one negation and no double negation, the negation would have to sit in the query. A TRUE label would then require the negation-free knowledge base to entail that negative query. The positive rules and facts in this pilot never entail a negative sentence, so the cell is empty."
    },
    {
      "cell": "RULE_ANTECEDENT label FALSE",
      "reason": "Proving a negative ground conclusion from a negated antecedent requires a second negative literal to discharge the antecedent. That would be a second negation, so the cell is empty."
    },
    {
      "cell": "RULE + QUANTIFIER or PROPOSITION label FALSE",
      "reason": "A negated universal in the knowledge base does not identify which domain element is the counterexample, so it does not by itself entail a negative ground atom. FALSE quantified items are queries that negate a stated universal."
    },
    {
      "cell": "FACT label FALSE at depth 3 or 5",
      "reason": "A negative ground fact is stated directly, so its reasoning depth is 0. It is not a one-step inference. Multi-hop FALSE examples use a negated rule consequent or a negated query."
    },
    {
      "cell": "QUERY + QUANTIFIER or PROPOSITION label FALSE above depth 0",
      "reason": "The knowledge base states the universal outright. The query is that statement's negation, so the proof is an axiom lookup of depth 0. Unused hops are not counted."
    },
    {
      "cell": "pair type query_polarity",
      "reason": "A query-polarity pair keeps one knowledge base and asks both q and NOT(q). Exactly one negation in every example forbids that pair: the positive query has zero negations when the base has none, and NOT(q) has two negations when the base already contains the target negation. Double negation is also excluded."
    }
  ],
